So CyberArk is not directly “logging in as DSRM.” It will use an authorized admin account to reset the DSRM password and login from CyberArk.
We will need a desginated account with access to do these on the server we are testing with.
This service account will be the logon account used by CyberArk
The DC will not be rebooted when CyberArk changes the password, hence Verify acction will be disabled

CyberArk Target Account:
  DSRM credential per DC

CyberArk Linked Account:
  Domain Admin or delegated DC admin account

CPM Service Account:
  Does not need to be Domain Admin if linked account is used
  Needs permission to run the TPC/PowerShell wrapper on CPM

DC Reboot:
  Not required for rotation
  Required only for real DSRM login testing

Validation:
  NTDSUTIL success output
  Security Event ID 4794 on the DC
